                                                                              The OSI and TCP/IP models

OSI model splits networking into layers. each layer is able to communicate only with the level above or below it. This allows for easy troubleshooting (" Is it a problem with a cable (L1)
or a DNS issue (L7)?").

OSI model (7 layers):
     No      |  Layer        |              Job                |           Protocols/Examples        |    Data unit (PDU)  |    Devices
      7      |  Application  | what the user's apps talk to    |    HTTP/S, DNS, FTP, SMTP, SSH      |    Data             |  (L7 firewall, proxy)
      6      |  Presentation | format, encrypt, compress       |    TLS/SSL, JPEG, ASCII             |    Data             |  -
      5      |  Session      | open, manage, close sessions    |    NetBIOS, RPC                     |    Data             |  -
      4      |  Transport    | ports, TCP/UDP, reliability     |    TCP, UDP                         |    Segment/datagram |  (stateful firewall)
      3      |  Network      | IP addresses, frames            |    IP, ICMP (ping)                  |    Packet           |  Router, L3 switch
      2      |  Data Link    | MAC addresses, frames           |    Ethernet, Wi-Fi(802.11), ARP     |    Frame            |  Switch, bridge, NIC, AP
      1      |  Physical     | bits on cable / radio           |    Cables, fibre, radio, signals    |    Bits             |  Hub, repeater, cables

TCP/IP model (4 layers) is what the internet actually runs on. OSI is the 7-layer "teaching" model used in exams and troubleshooting.

                                                                                    Encapsulation
Encapsulation is how data gets wrapped n the way down:
L7-5 Data              [---------------------------------------DATA---------------------------------------]
L4 Segment             [--TCP/UDP hdr ports--][--------------------------DATA-----------------------------]
L3 Packet              [--IP hdr IPs--][--TCP/UDP hdr--][---------------------DATA------------------------]
L2 Frame               [--Eth hdr MACs--][--IP hdr IPs--][--TCP/UDP hdr--][-----DATA-----][--FCS trailer--]
L1 Bits                [----------------------------0110100101001011010010110..---------------------------]
Sending = encapsulation (add a header at each layer going down).
Recieving = de-encapsulation  (strip them off goign up).


                                                                              LANs, topologies and devices


A LAN (Local Area Network) is a network in one place, like a home or an office.
A WAN (Wide Area Network) connects LANs over long distances (the internet is the biggest WAN)

Star - every device plugs into a central switch. Most common. easy to add devices; if the switch dies, everything stops
Bus - everything shares one backbone cable. Cheap, but traffic jams and one break takes the network down.
Ring - each device links to the next; data goes one way round. Little cabling, but one break or faulty device stops the loop.

       Device        |  OSI layer  |                                  What it does
  Hub                |      1      |  Repeats every signal out of every port. Dumb, insecure (everyone sees everything), obsolete
  Switch             |      2      |  Learns MAC addresses and send frames only to the right port. Can split a LAN into VLANs.
  Router             |      3      |  Connects different networks and picks the path using IP addresses. Your home router is also your default gateway.
  Firewall           |     3-7     |  Allows or blocks traffic based on the rules (IP, port, protocol, app)
  Access point (AP)  |      2      |  Bridges Wi-Fi devices onto the wired LAN.
  NIC                |     1-2     |  Network card in each device; has a burned-in MAC address (48 bits, e.g. a4:83:e7:2b:10:9f).
  Load balancer      |     4-7     |  Spreads traffic across several servers so none is overloaded.


                                                                      Packets, TCP and UDP

              |                      TCP                      |          UDP            
Conncetion    |  Yes (handshake first)                        |  No
Reliable?     |  Yes: numbered, acknowledged, resent if lost  |  No: fire and forget
Order         |  Guaranteed                                   |  Not guaranteed
Speed         |  Slower (more overhead)                       |  Faster, lighter
Used for      |  Web, email, SSH, file transfer               |  DNS queries, DHCP, streaming,  VoIP, gaming

3-way handshake

1. Client sends SYN request to server
2. Server replies with SYN/ACK
3. Client replies wiht ACK

Closing uses FIN/ACK
RST kills a connection abruptly

TCP flag      |      Meaning
SYN           |  Start a connection (synchronise sequence numbers)
ACK           |  Acknowledge data recieved
FIN           |  Finished: close the connection politely
RST           |  Reset: close it immediately (often a closed port answering a scan)
PSH/URG       |  Push data to the app now / urgent data

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Security angle
Port scanners line NMAP send SYNs and read the reply: 
SYN/ACK = Port open
RST = closed
nothing = filtered by firewall
A SYN flood sends thousands of SYNs and never Acks, to fill uo the server.
