                                                                                        ARP and DHCP


ARP: IP -> MAC (Layer 2/3)
On a LAN, frames are delivered by MAC address, so a device needs the MAC that matches and IP.
  1. Request (broadcast to everyone): "Who has 192.168.1.1? Tell 192.168.1.20."
  2. Reply (unicast): "192.168.1.1 is at a4:83:e7:2b:1-:9f."
  3. The answer is saved in ARP cache (see it with arp -a).
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Security:
ARP has no authentication, so an attacker can send fake replies (ARP spoofing / poisoning) to sit in the middle of traffic.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

DHCP: automatic IP settings (UDP 67/68)
Remember it as DORA:
Step  |          Who          |        Message
  D   |  Client -> all        |  Discover: "Any DHCP servers?"
  O   |  Server -> client     |  Offer: "You can have .25"
  R   |  Client -> all        |  Request: I'll take .25"
  A   |  Server -> client     |  Acknowledge: "It's yours"
Gives:
IP address, subnet mask, default gateway, DNS server, lease time.
No answer -> the device gives itself an APIPA address (169.254.x.x).


                                                                          Binary and IPv4 addressing


Each octet is 8 bits. Every bit has a place value; add up the ones that are switched on.

Place value    |  128  |  64  |  32  |  16  |  8  |  4  |  2  |  1  |        =Decimal
    192        |   1   |   1  |   0  |   0  |  0  |  0  |  0  |  0  |        128+64=192
    168        |   1   |   0  |   1  |   0  |  1  |  0  |  0  |  0  |        128+32+8=168
    172        |   1   |   0  |   1  |   0  |  1  |  1  |  0  |  0  |        128+32+8+4=172
    10         |   0   |   0  |   0  |   0  |  1  |  0  |  1  |  0  |        8+2=10
    255        |   1   |   1  |   1  |   1  |  1  |  1  |  1  |  1  |        all on = 255
Decimal -> binary: start at 128. If the number is more or equal the place value, write 1 and subtract; otherwise write 0. Move right and repeat.

- IPv4 = 32 bits, 4 octets ~4.3 billion addresses (not enough, hence NAT and IPv6)
- Subnet mask = which bits are network vs host. 255.255.255.0 = /24 (CIDR notation counts are 1-bits)
- Default gateway = the router's IP; where traffic goes when the destination is outside your subnet.
- NAT = the router swaps private IPs for tis one public IP on the way out.
- Unicast: one-to-one
- Broadcast: one-to-all on the subnet
- Multicast: one-to-a-group

Special range    |          CIDR          |      USE
Private A        |  10.0.0.0/8            |  Internal
Private B        |  172.16.0.0/12         |  Internal (172.16-32)
Private C        |  192.168.0.0/16        |  Internal
Loopback         |  127.0.0.0/8           |  Own machine
APIPA            |  169.254.0.0/16        |  DHCP failed

Old address classes (stil asked in exams)

Class    |    First octet    |        Default mask          |                USE
  A      |      1 - 126      |    /8 (255.0.0.0)            |  Very large networks
  B      |     128 - 191     |    /16 (255.255.0.0)         |  Medium networks
  C      |     192 - 223     |    /24 (255.255.255.0)       |  Small networks
  D      |     224 - 239     |              -               |  Multicast
  E      |     240 - 255     |              -               |  Experimental / reserved
127 is missing from Class A because it's reserved for loopback. Modern networks use CIDR instead of classes.


                                                                                      Subnetting (Short)


Full dtails are located in subnetting cheat sheet. The essentials:

Formula          |  Meaning                              |              Magic number in 5 steps
32 - prefix      |  Host bits                            |  1. Find the interesting octet (mask is not 255/00)
2^host bits - 2  |  Usable hosts                         |  2. Magic number = 256 - mask value
2^subnet bits    |  Number of subnets                    |  3. Network = biggest multiple ≤ the IP's octet.
256 - mask value |  Magic number (block size)            |  4. Broadcast = next multiple -1
/25  |  /26  |  /27  |  /28  |  /29  |  /30              |  5. Hosts = Network +1 to broadcast -1
128  |  192  |  224  |  240  |  248  |  252              |  Example: 192.168.10.77/27 -> magic = 32 -> network .64, broadcast .95, hosts .65-.94 (30)
126h |  62h  |  30h  |  14h  |  6h   |  2h               


                                                                                Extending your network


  Concept          |   What it is                                                                                                      |  Example
Port forwarding    |  The router sends traffic arriving on a public port to a specific internal device and port                        |  public :80 -> 192.168.1.10:80 to host a web server at home. Opens a door fromt he internet, so only forward what you need
Firewall           |  Checks traffic against rules: where from, where to, which port, which protocol, Allow or deny                    |  Hardware (a box on the network) or software (Windows Defender Firewall, iptables).
VPN                |  An encrypted tunnel across an untrusted network (the internet), so remote devices act an if they're on the LAN.  |  Site-to-site (Office <-> office) or remote access (laptop <-> office). Protocols: IPsec, OpenVPN, WireGuard; old PPTP is insecure.
VLAN               |  Splits one physical switch into separate virtual LANs that can't talk without a router/firewall                  |  Guests, staff and server on different VLANs = segmentation, which limits how far an attacker can move.

  Stateless firewall                                |  Stateful firewall
Checks each packet on its own against the rules     |  Tracks whole connections (rememembers the handshake)
Fast, uses fewer resources                          |  Smarter: auto-allows replies to connections you started 
Easier to fool; good against floods of junk         |  Uses more resources; the cormal choice today


                                                                        DNS: the internet's phonebook (port 53)


DNS turnns names (www.example.com) into IP addresses. The namespace is a tree, read right to left:
    www      |        example        |    .com      |      .        
  subdomain  |  second-level domain  |     TLD      |    root

How a lookup looks:                                                              
                        1                       2  Root server                  
                       -->                      /  3                            
Your PC (checks cache)      Recursive resolver    -  .com TLD                  
                       <--                      \                               
                        5                       4  Authoritative (example.com)  

1. PC asks its resolver (ISP/router/8.8.8.8)
2. root says "ask .com"
3. TLD says "ask example.com's server"
4. authoritative server gives the IP
5. answer returned and cached for its TTL

|      Record      |  Holds
|  A               |  IPv4 address
|  AAAA            |  IPv6 address
|  CNAME           |  Alias to another name
|  MX              |  Mail server for the domain (with priority)
|  TXT             |  Free text: SPF/DKIM, domain verifiication
|  NS              |  Which servers are authoritative
|  PTR             |  Reverse lookup: IP -> name

Try it:
nslookup example.com (Windows)
dig example.com (Linux)
DNS uses UDP 54 for normal queries, TCP 53 for big repies and zone transfers.


